.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt GEFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gefs ,
.Nm newfs_gefs ,
.Nm fsck_gefs
.Nd make, check and administer gefs file systems
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm newfs_gefs
.Op Fl 9
.Op Fl r Ar spec
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Ar special
.Nm fsck_gefs
.Op Fl fpy
.Ar special
.Nm
.Cm status
.Ar path | special
.Nm
.Cm snap
.Op Fl m
.Ar path | special
.Ar label new
.Nm
.Cm fork
.Ar path | special
.Ar label new
.Nm
.Cm delsnap
.Ar path | special
.Ar label
.Nm
.Cm retain
.Ar path | special
.Ar label | Cm \-
.Ar spec ...
.Nm
.Cm sync
.Ar path
.Nm
.Cm check
.Ar path | special
.Nm
.Cm ctl
.Ar path | special
.Ar command ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
gefs is the copy-on-write file system of 9front
.Pq Xr gefs 4 there ,
a B-epsilon tree whose snapshots are free to take and cost only the
space of what changed since.
A gefs file system is a set of trees, each named by a label.
.Dq main
is the one mounted by default;
.Dq empty
is the empty tree every other is forked from, and
.Dq adm
holds 9front's user table, which
.Nx
does not use.
A
.Em fork
is a mutable label; a
.Em snapshot
is a read-only one.
Any label may be mounted
.Pq Xr mount_gefs 8 ,
several at once.
.Pp
The administration commands work on a mounted file system, given any
.Ar path
on it, through an
.Xr ioctl 2 ;
or on a
.Ar special
device or image file that is not mounted, which
.Nm
then loads and writes itself.
They are the commands of 9front's
.Pa /adm/ctl :
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm status
Print the space used, the retention settings, and the labels, each as
.Dq fork
or
.Dq snap .
.It Cm snap Oo Fl m Oc Ar label new
Take a snapshot of
.Ar label
named
.Ar new ;
with
.Fl m ,
or as
.Cm fork ,
a mutable one.
For a mounted label, the files' dirty pages go into the snapshot.
.It Cm delsnap Ar label
Delete a label, freeing the blocks nothing else holds.
A mounted label cannot be deleted.
Deleting a fork leaves the snapshots taken of it.
.It Cm retain Ar label spec ...
Set the automatic snapshots of
.Ar label ,
or with
.Cm \-
the default for the labels that set none.
A
.Ar spec
is a list of
.Ar count Ns Li @ Ns Ar interval Ns Ar unit ,
the unit
.Li m ,
.Li h
or
.Li d :
.Dq 60@m 24@h 14@d
keeps a snapshot for each of the last 60 minutes, 24 hours and 14 days.
Without a count, snapshots are kept forever; without an interval, it is
1.
.Cm none
takes no automatic snapshots (and removes the label's existing ones);
.Cm default
clears the label's own setting.
A fork inherits the setting of the label it was forked from.
.It Cm sync
Commit everything now.
.It Cm check
Check the whole file system.
.It Cm ctl Ar command
Run any of the above, as a command line of 9front's
.Pa /adm/ctl ,
and
.Cm usereserve 1 ,
which lets deletions use the space kept in reserve when the file system
is full.
.El
.Pp
Automatic snapshots are named
.Ar label Ns Li @ Ns Ar tier Ns Li \&. Ns Ar YYYY.MM.DD_hh:mm:ss ,
in UTC, the tier
.Li minute ,
.Li hour
or
.Li day .
Unlike 9front, which takes one each interval, a snapshot is taken by a
commit that writes anyway, and only when the label changed since that
tier's last one: an idle file system does no I/O, and the snapshots
kept are states that differ.
.Pp
.Nm newfs_gefs
creates a file system on
.Ar special :
a root directory owned by root, and the default retention
.Dq 60@m 24@h 14@d
.Pq Fl r Ar spec No to choose another, Fl r Cm none No for none .
With
.Fl 9 ,
the file system is made as 9front's
.Nm gefs Fl r
makes it (a root owned by adm, no retention setting, so 9front's default
.Dq 60@m 24@h @d ,
which keeps a daily snapshot forever);
.Fl u
names the user 9front's user table gets.
.Pp
.Nm fsck_gefs
checks the trees, the free lists and the deadlists.
gefs needs no repair after a crash: a mount finds the last commit.
The options of
.Xr fsck 8
are accepted and ignored.
.Sh BOOTING
The
.Nx
EFI boot loader reads gefs: it loads
.Pa boot.cfg ,
the kernel and modules from the GPT partition marked
.Dq bootme
.Pq Xr gpt 8 ,
and a path starting with
.Li @ Ns Ar label
is read from that label, say an automatic snapshot, to boot yesterday's
kernel:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
boot @main@day.2026.09.27_00:00:00/netbsd
.Ed
.Pp
A kernel with gefs built in finds its root from the boot loader and
mounts
.Dq main ,
or the label it was itself loaded from: a boot environment.
Fork the system before an upgrade, and boot the fork if the upgrade
goes wrong:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gefs fork / main pre-upgrade
\&... upgrade ...
boot @pre-upgrade/netbsd	(at the boot loader's prompt)
.Ed
.Pp
A fork runs read-write as the root; a snapshot runs read-only.
A
.Xr boot.cfg 5
line makes it a menu entry:
.Dl menu=Before the upgrade:boot @pre-upgrade/netbsd
.Sh EXAMPLES
Make a gefs root partition that the EFI loader boots from:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpt add -a 1m -t 38417555-670b-4a70-8e7a-bd7ce366f5a1 -l gefsroot wd0
gpt set -a bootme -i 2 wd0
newfs_gefs /dev/rdk1
.Ed
.Pp
Keep build output out of the snapshots of
.Dq main :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gefs fork / empty obj
gefs retain / obj none
mount -t gefs -o snap=obj /dev/dk1 /usr/obj
.Ed
.Pp
Take a snapshot before an upgrade, and look at it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gefs snap / main pre-upgrade
mount -t gefs -o ro,snap=pre-upgrade /dev/dk1 /mnt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr gpt 8 ,
.Xr mount_gefs 8
.Rs
.%A Ori Bernstein
.%T GEFS, a good enough file system
.Re
.Sh HISTORY
gefs was written by Ori Bernstein for 9front.
The
.Nx
port appeared in 2026.
.Sh BUGS
There is no index from inode numbers to files, so gefs file systems
cannot be exported over NFS.
Change times are the modification times.
Extended attributes are not supported.
